    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Following the 2019 protests and the passage of the National Security Law, the Hong Kong SAR government and Central Government officials re-evaluated the elements of Hong Kong society believed to be cultivating unpatriotic thought. Among the policies targeted was the required civic\r\neducation subject Liberal Studies. The 2021 reform remarketed the subject as Citizenship and Social Development with the expressed purpose of instilling Chinese national identity within young people and clarifying the duties of Chinese citizenship for those with Hong Kong residency. Wu (2021) offers a historical understanding of civic education by analyzing the discrepancies between commercial and government Liberal Studies textbooks—denoted as the boundaries of official knowledge. Theorized by Apple (1993), this concept elucidates how political, economic, and socio-cultural factors inform which knowledge is legitimated and represented in curriculum, namely via textbooks. Expanding upon Wu’s work, this thesis considers the current relationship among government actors, curriculum, and the new boundaries of official knowledge in a post-2019 context. It specifically poses the question: how does the HKSAR government presently communicate Chinese citizenship to senior secondary students through national education? Pursuing a qualitative content analysis study, the thesis works with a data set of 17 Education Bureau press releases, one Curriculum and Assessment Guide and six CSD textbooks that were reviewed and approved by the Hong Kong Education Bureau. Following a period of data immersion, a combination of deductive and in vivo codes was produced and applied in multiple passes for analysis. Through thematic organizing and frequency notation, the historical narratives of Hong Kong and the profile of a model Chinese citizen were constructed. The results suggest that the HKSAR government’s broader programmatic efforts to develop a model citizenry in the city has initiated a fifth era of reform in Hong Kong civic education to national education—where the city’s compatibility with China’s ethnic and political cultures are emphasized.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
